.TH "WIILAND" 7 "August 2026" "WiiLand" "Wii Remote Driver"
.SH NAME
WiiLand \- Linux Wii Remote input stack for Wayland and X.org sessions
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
WiiLand connects Nintendo Wii Remotes to Linux through BlueZ, the kernel
.B hid-wiimote
driver, and the native Rust
.B wiiland-hid
device library.  The
.B wiilandd (1)
daemon reads those interfaces and publishes display-neutral Linux
.B uinput
virtual evdev devices.  This is one operating model for both Wayland and X.org
sessions; X.org is not a legacy-only path.
.
.SS Layered input path
The path is:
.IP 1. 4
hid-wiimote and BlueZ expose the paired remote; wiiland-hid supplies decoded
buttons, extension data, accelerometer, MotionPlus, IR, and Balance Board data.
.IP 2. 4
wiilandd creates virtual devices through /dev/uinput.  The gamepad profile is
named
.B "WiiLand Virtual Controller"
and the desktop profile
.B "WiiLand Virtual Desktop" .
.IP 3. 4
A Wayland compositor consumes those evdev devices through libinput.  An X.org
server consumes the same devices through its normal input stack.  XWayland
clients receive seat input forwarded by the Wayland compositor.
.IP 4. 4
Native Wayland, native X11, XWayland, SDL, Steam Input, and Wine/Proton
applications consume the resulting controller, pointer, and keyboard events
through their ordinary interfaces.
.PP
uinput is shared because it is a kernel input boundary rather than a
display-protocol injection API.  The same wiilandd output is consequently
available to either session without compositor-specific plugins or separate
decoding paths.
.
.SS X.org raw-device policy
For a native X.org installation, the X.org integration provides
.B res/50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf
with InputClass rules that suppress matching raw Nintendo Wii Remote event
nodes.  This prevents duplicate pointer input from raw accelerometer/IR axes.
It does not block wiiland-hid or wiilandd, and it does not suppress devices
named
.B "WiiLand Virtual Controller"
or
.B "WiiLand Virtual Desktop" .
The Classic Controller and Pro Controller exceptions remain enabled.
This policy has no effect on a Wayland session.
.
.SH PAIRING
Pair from the desktop Bluetooth UI or with the current
.B bluetoothctl
workflow.  BlueZ manages pairing and the kernel
.B hid-wiimote
driver exposes paired remotes; no additional kernel configuration is required.
.PP
.RS
.B wiilandd --list --verbose
.RE
.PP
to list connected devices, devtypes, and extension attributes without creating
virtual devices.
.
.SH OPERATION
The default backend is
.B uinput
and the default profile is
.B gamepad .
Use
.B "wiilandd --profile desktop"
for pointer/keyboard output or
.B "wiilandd --profile both"
for both virtual devices.  Desktop mode maps valid IR and D-pad repeat to
pointer motion, A/B to clicks, +/- to Enter/Escape, Home to overview, and 1/2
to Page Down/Page Up.  Motion aiming is selected with
.B aim-mode=mouse
or
.B aim-mode=right-stick ;
.B aim-source=auto
prefers IR, then calibrated MotionPlus, then calibrated accelerometer tilt.
.PP
The observable names were changed from
.B "WiiLand Wayland Controller"
and
.B "WiiLand Wayland Desktop"
to
.B "WiiLand Virtual Controller"
and
.B "WiiLand Virtual Desktop" .
Applications that saved the former names may require device remapping.
.
.SH SESSIONS
.SS Wayland
The compositor reads wiilandd's virtual evdev devices through libinput.  Native
Wayland applications and XWayland applications then use their normal seat
interfaces.  XWayland is not required by wiilandd.
.SS X.org
The X.org server reads the same virtual evdev devices.  Install the raw-node
InputClass policy described above when raw Wii Remote axes would otherwise
produce duplicate pointer input; this policy does not suppress virtual devices.
.SS Shared applications
SDL, Steam Input, Wine/Proton, and native games read the gamepad profile
through Linux evdev or their usual input backend in either session.  Desktop
pointer and keyboard output follows the session's normal seat.
.
.SH FRONTENDS
The optional
.B wiiland-config
control center is a Rust eframe/egui application.  Its native winit backends
support Wayland and X11, and its application ID is
.B io.github.philosophimoonbeam.wiiland-config .
No toolkit-specific platform override is required.  The model preserves
canonical daemon values, revision/transaction ownership, atomic same-directory
saves, service controls, and stale asynchronous-completion protection.  Trace
and calibration actions are mutually exclusive.  Fixed-width command output
retains the most recent 10,000 text blocks.
.PP
Live trace and calibration normally use daemon IPC 1.1, keeping virtual input
active. The GUI also offers explicit direct-hardware diagnostics with the
service stopped.
.PP
The optional
.B wiiland-show
diagnostic is a ratatui/crossterm TUI.  It supports 80x24 minimum and 160x48
extended layouts, full controller displays, and terminal cleanup on exit. It uses daemon IPC by default; q quits and f freezes.
Use --direct with the daemon stopped for hardware controls, or --socket PATH
for a custom daemon endpoint.
.
.SH PERMISSIONS
wiilandd must read the hid-wiimote
.B /dev/input/event*
nodes and write
.B /dev/uinput .
Install
.B res/70-udev-wiiland.rules
and
.B res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules
or the packaged equivalents.  The latter rule uses
.B TAG+="uaccess"
for active-seat input access and
.B GROUP="input", MODE="0660"
as a fallback; the former grants group write access to Wii Remote LED
brightness attributes.  No display-server client permission is needed.
.
.SH SERVICE
The packaged
.B wiilandd.service
is a systemd user unit installed in the selected user-unit directory and
pulled by
.B default.target .
Its ExecCondition runs
.B "wiilandd --check-config"
before ExecStart, so invalid configuration suppresses startup without entering
the runtime restart policy.  A process that starts and fails is restarted with
.B Restart=on-failure
and
.BR RestartSec=2s .
The unit also denies privilege gain, personality changes, writable executable
memory, real-time scheduling, SUID/SGID changes, and foreign syscall
architectures, and uses
.BR UMask=0077 .
Enable and start it with:
.PP
.RS
.B "systemctl --user enable wiilandd.service"
.br
.B "systemctl --user start wiilandd.service"
.RE
.PP
Use
.B "systemctl --user status wiilandd.service"
for lifecycle diagnostics.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Unless
.B --no-config
is supplied, wiilandd loads the system configuration from
.BR /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
first, then an absolute
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
or
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset or relative.  Later values override earlier
values.
.B --config path
selects one explicit key=value file instead of that hierarchy, and command
line options override loaded values.
.B --config
and
.B --no-config
cannot be combined.  Use
.B --check-config
to validate without enumerating devices and
.B --dump-config
to print the resolved result.  The packaged
.B res/wiilandd.conf
is a sample/default file, not an additional implicit layer.
.
.SH INSTALLATION
The repository pins Rust 1.98.0 and uses a Cargo workspace.  Build and install
the release profile with:
.PP
.RS
.B "cargo xtask build --release --features gui,tui,integrations"
.br
.B "cargo xtask install --release --prefix /usr --sysconfdir /etc \e"
.br
.B "  --features gui,tui,integrations"
.RE
.PP
The xtask build compiles the selected Cargo workspace binaries and libraries
into the requested target profile.
.PP
Pass
.B --destdir PATH
to stage the same logical tree.  The typed xtask manifest installs
.B wiilandd ,
.B wiilandd-hardware-report ,
optional frontend binaries, configuration, documentation, manual pages, and
selected integration assets.
.PP
The integration options
.B --with-udev-rules-dir ,
.B --with-systemd-user-unit-dir ,
and
.B --with-xorg-conf-dir
each accept
.B auto ,
.B no ,
or an absolute destination.  Generated service content uses logical paths;
DESTDIR is not embedded in it.  The default logical prefix is /usr/local.
.
.SH VALIDATION
Hardware-free checks are:
.PP
.RS
.B wiilandd --self-test
.br
.B wiilandd --check-config
.br
.B wiilandd --dump-config
.br
.B wiilandd --axis-map
.br
.B wiilandd --validation-checklist
.br
.B wiilandd --doctor
.br
.B wiilandd-hardware-report
.RE
.PP
With a paired remote, select the number from
.B --list
or a sysfs path and capture dry-run traces:
.PP
.RS
.B "wiilandd-hardware-report N"
.br
.B "wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=motion-plus"
.br
.B "wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=ir"
.RE
.PP
After traces are correct, stop the user service if it is running and create
live devices with:
.PP
.RS
.B "wiilandd --device N --profile both"
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, set
.B profile=both
in the effective configuration and restart the user service.  Record paired
hardware and consumer results for: original Wii Remote; original remote plus
external MotionPlus; Wii Remote Plus; Nunchuk; Classic Controller; Wii U Pro
Controller; Guitar; Drums; and Balance Board.  For each applicable profile,
validate native Wayland, native X.org, XWayland, SDL, one Steam Input game, one
native or XWayland non-Steam game, and one Wine/Proton game.  Exercise desktop
pointer/buttons and both motion modes where applicable.  Record kernel and
wiilandd versions, distribution, Bluetooth adapter, devtype, extension state,
session, consumer, and observed pass/fail details.  A source checkout or
hardware-free run does not constitute physical hardware validation.
.
.SH BUGS
If a remote is absent, inspect
.B "wiilandd --list --verbose"
and
.B "wiilandd --doctor"
before checking Bluetooth, hid-wiimote, udev permissions, and the user-unit
status.  For X.org duplicate pointer events, verify the raw-node InputClass
policy is installed and that the virtual device names are not being ignored.
.
